TASK 11 – Recommend and Defend the Final Design
4 Marks
1. Recommended Algorithm
Recommended approach: Hash Table Search using Separate Chaining.

For the university student-record system, Hash Table Search is recommended because registration numbers are unique identifiers and students need to be located quickly.
2. Reasons for Selecting Hash Table Search
1. It provides fast average search performance.
2. It does not require sorting registration numbers.
3. It directly calculates the bucket index using a hash function.
4. It is suitable for frequent student-record searches.
5. It can handle collisions using separate chaining.
3. Theoretical Efficiency
For n student records:

Average-case search:
\[
T(n)=\Theta(1)
\]

Worst-case search:
\[
T(n)=O(n)
\]

Hash-table construction:
\[
P(n)=O(n)
\]

The average search time remains approximately constant when the hash function distributes registration numbers evenly and the load factor is controlled.
4. Experimental Performance
The experimental results from Task 9 show the following comparison.
Input size	Linear comparisons	Binary comparisons	Hash comparisons
1,000	1,000	10	1
10,000	10,000	14	1
100,000	100,000	17	1
1,000,000	1,000,000	20	1

The Hash Table Search performed one comparison for the successful searches in the test dataset because the selected hash-table size prevented collisions.

This supports selecting Hash Table Search for frequent registration-number lookups.
5. Preprocessing Cost
Hash Table Search requires an initial construction process.
- All registration numbers must be inserted into the hash table.
- A hash function calculates the index for every registration number.
- Collisions are handled using separate chaining.
The average construction complexity is:
\[
O(n)
\]

Although construction requires additional time and memory, it is worthwhile when the database is searched repeatedly.
6. Trade-offs and Limitations
Advantage	Limitation
Fast average search	Requires extra memory
No sorting required	Hash collisions may occur
Suitable for frequent searches	Requires hash-table maintenance
Fast registration-number lookup	Worst case can become O(n)

7. Practicality for Growing Databases
The recommended approach remains practical as the database grows from thousands to millions of student records.

A suitable hash function, sufficient bucket capacity and collision handling help maintain efficient searching.

When new student records are added, they can be inserted into the hash table without sorting the entire database.
8. Final Recommendation
Hash Table Search with Separate Chaining is recommended for the university student-record system. Its average search complexity is O(1), and the experimental comparison showed fewer registration-number comparisons than Linear Search and Binary Search.

Although it requires additional memory and table construction, these costs are justified when many searches are performed. Therefore, it provides a suitable balance between search speed, preprocessing and database maintenance.
